The binding shape is explicit in `BOOK_MAP.md:112`: “the ruling in the year's last month; the Line crossed after the year's close.” The last-month row says “Closing sittings; Ilsev; the ruling; the council; the last bout” (`BOOK_MAP.md:122`), followed by “Four days before the year's close → after the close” (`BOOK_MAP.md:123`). M8 enters in “The academic year's last month. Two closing sittings at Ostrand. Brom's review on the year's last Fifth-day” (`packets/MOVEMENT-008.md:13`). M9 enters “The morning after the last bout, four days before the academic year's close” (`packets/MOVEMENT-009.md:13`) and must end after the Line (`packets/MOVEMENT-009.md:36`).

The day-115 constraint is already fixed by the coordinator: “day 75 → day 115” (`STATE_LEDGER.md:905`) and “The clean door's forty-day default counts from the close of the fourth sitting” (`STATE_LEDGER.md:987`). Nothing in the recess or later sitting dates changes it.

Brom's ninety days do not require the panel itself to sit by day 22. The governing coordinator ruling says the docket “convened under the docket of the fourth day” answers the summons inside the ninety days, while the hearing remains the last Fifth-day (`STATE_LEDGER.md:446`; see also `STATE_LEDGER.md:207`). The page preserves the same-day binding: “The district has had a standard panel listed for that day since the fourth” (`manuscript/chapter-47.md:107`) and “The same day” (`manuscript/chapter-47.md:109–111`).

### 1. The calendar — RATIFY

**Page.** The recess begins after the day-98 Seventh-day entry: “The recess starts tomorrow” (`manuscript/chapter-41.md:45–49`), and the term resumes on a First-day (`manuscript/chapter-43.md:237`). The sixth sitting is tied to day 117 by Havel's schedule comparison: “re-tendered on the hundred and seventeenth” (`manuscript/chapter-44.md:201`). The seat sets “Two closing sittings, in the last month … a week apart” and the ruling for its “last Fifth-day” (`manuscript/chapter-47.md:93`); the month opens on First-day (`manuscript/chapter-47.md:165`), and Bracken posts the second, third, and last Fifth-days (`manuscript/chapter-47.md:173–181`).

**Governing text.** `BOOK_MAP.md:121–123` requires the recess/long corridor, the last-month ruling, and the post-close Line in that order. `STATE_LEDGER.md:446` fixes the early sitting and inventory clocks; `STATE_LEDGER.md:683–694` fixes day 71, day 75, and day 76; `STATE_LEDGER.md:905,915,987` fixes day 115. The M8/M9 packet inheritance is quoted above.

**Ruling.** All supplied dates stand. Day 201 is a Fifth-day and remains the month's final Fifth-day under either permitted M8/M9 finish (year close day 206 or 207); the next Fifth-day is day 208. Bracken's “expect fourteen” also closes exactly: eleven merits sittings, two closings, one ruling. The supplied calendar does not itself date the last bout, so M8 must choose day 201 or 202 and then carry the corresponding M9 entry/year-close pair without mixing them.

### 2. The Velmere envelope — RATIFY THE COORDINATOR OVERRIDE

**Page.** Brom removes a single envelope that has been carried “since the day it came” and is rubbed from “eighteen weeks” in his pocket; “The seal had not been broken” (`manuscript/chapter-47.md:129–137`). He squares it on the table “Four weeks … To the day” before the ruling (`manuscript/chapter-47.md:137–147`) and says, “I'm not opening it before the panel and I'm not opening it before the ruling” (`manuscript/chapter-47.md:151`).

**Governing text.** The coordinator says: “THE VELMERE LETTER (day 45) stays SEALED … it is BOOK_MAP §4e #10's unopened letter for M7” (`STATE_LEDGER.md:563`), carries it sealed through M5 (`STATE_LEDGER.md:808`) and M6 (`STATE_LEDGER.md:909,920`), and overrides the looser §4e wording about an arrival four weeks before the ruling. M8 assumes “The second Velmere envelope unopened” (`packets/MOVEMENT-008.md:13`) and further requires that Brom “does not open the envelope” (`packets/MOVEMENT-008.md:32`). M9 makes no contrary assumption.

**Ruling.** The envelope on the table is the day-45 letter. No new envelope arrives in M7. “Four weeks” dates its move to the table, not its arrival. This is the exact coordinator-controlled reconciliation of `BOOK_MAP.md:444–446`.

### 3. Gault's gate note — RATIFY

**Page.** Gault identifies the “leaf for the year's seventy-sixth day” (`manuscript/chapter-46.md:35`); the clerk reads the riders and unnotified assessment into the record (`manuscript/chapter-46.md:43–45`); Jent says, “The petitioner has no knowledge of the matter in that note … He relies on none of it” (`manuscript/chapter-46.md:49–51`); the hood answers, “Received … And entered” (`manuscript/chapter-46.md:53`).

**Governing text.** The day-76 gate is fixed in the closed M4 ledger (`STATE_LEDGER.md:683,694,743`). The road attack remains unreported (`STATE_LEDGER.md:814,852`), while `BOOK_MAP.md:638` permits only the two already-completed faction moves and forbids a third. M8 requires “nobody dishonest anywhere” and keeps Jent counsel, not a faction face (`packets/MOVEMENT-008.md:17,34`).

**Ruling.** Entering a truthful note about the first move is not a third move and does not report the day-82 road cut. Jent's disclaimer protects rather than disturbs M8: it records the separation between the petition and the ownerless instrument without naming or locating the faction.

### 4. Vastin's window — RATIFY M7; CARRY THE M8 ROLE-LABEL CAUTION

**Page.** The renewed request says first notice was given “in the year's eighth week” and asks the Archmarshal's office to certify before the first closing (`manuscript/chapter-46.md:253–265`). Vastin writes only, “*The Archmarshal declines to certify.*” (`manuscript/chapter-46.md:275–281`). He knows “The code let a seat proceed upon its own authority where a certificate was withheld” (`manuscript/chapter-46.md:283`).

**Governing text.** M7 calls for “the proceedings' request for certification renewed; he declines in one line; nothing else” (`packets/MOVEMENT-007.md:22`) and limits him to “one line of declination” as the operative act (`packets/MOVEMENT-007.md:38`). His POV limit forbids a report, file, alliance, departure, mechanism knowledge, or theory of the layer (`BOOK_MAP.md:491`). M8 protects the exact minute: “*Evaluation-protocol certification: declined by the evaluator of record; proceeding on the presiding seat's authority.*” (`BOOK_MAP.md:613–614`; `packets/MOVEMENT-008.md:29`). The ledger separately fixes “Magister Gault evaluator of record” (`BOOK_MAP.md:22`; `STATE_LEDGER.md:84`).

**Ruling.** M7 supplies every fact the protected M8 minute needs: a refusal, a withheld certificate, and statutory authority to proceed. It adds no forbidden act. M8 must quote its Tier B line verbatim, while its surrounding narration must preserve the distinct offices: Gault is Halcenvane's evaluator of record; Vastin is the Archmarshal who withholds certification. This is the audit's sole carry caution.

### 5. Havel — RATIFY

**Page.** Havel's assigned comparison requires him to read both leaves line by line (`manuscript/chapter-44.md:141–151`), so the designation's title necessarily passes under his pen (`manuscript/chapter-44.md:153–159`). He considers for “perhaps a breath” whether it might be a seventh (`manuscript/chapter-44.md:197–201`), then rules it answerable by the registers and writes nothing (`manuscript/chapter-44.md:201–207`). He notices “*until relieved*” but does not yet ask (`manuscript/chapter-44.md:209–213`).

**Governing text.** Havel may know “The Level 4 marker he was told to stand down about; the unplaceable seal; his own note,” but “Never connects the seal or the flag to falsification. The note stops at six entries” (`BOOK_MAP.md:493`). M7 requires “he rereads six entries; he does not write a seventh” (`packets/MOVEMENT-007.md:22,38`), and M8 owns “the transfer request; six entries, done” (`packets/MOVEMENT-008.md:22`).

**Ruling.** Knowledge of the title is not knowledge of falsification. Havel connects the title only to its openly receipted schedule line, explicitly rejects it as a seventh gap, and keeps six sheets. *Until relieved* is the correct plant for M8's transfer request.

### 6. Lira's window — RATIFY

**Page.** The three-sentence Kindling memory is: “She remembered a brass plate, and cold fingers turning her by the shoulder, and being told to stand still. She did not remember anybody saying a word she could use” (`manuscript/chapter-43.md:195–197`). She immediately refuses to invent a conclusion from it (`manuscript/chapter-43.md:199–201`).

**Governing text.** Lira may know “Everything she has seen; the mechanism (since Book 1); the cache from M6; Fenmark as given,” but not “Her life before Fenmark beyond canon” (`BOOK_MAP.md:492`). Earlier canon already places this event at Fenmark and gives substantially more of it: “At Fenmark she had been fourteen … She had put her hand where a clerk pointed” (`../book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-60.md:129`), with the result Copper Rank One; the B4 ledger also fixes the reading at about a minute and a half (`../book-04-copper-crown/STATE_LEDGER.md:231`).

**Ruling.** The memory is not pre-Fenmark history. It is a sensory restatement of her already-canon Fenmark Kindling and releases no new truth about falsification, family, or earlier life. Its final refusal to theorize keeps it below the §9 ceiling.

### 7. The night spar — RATIFY

**Page.** Cael prices “Everything public, at the rate, to win” and excludes “the quiet thing” and “the new one” (`manuscript/chapter-45.md:227`). The door is shut, Lira alone is present, and he lets the managed rate fall (`manuscript/chapter-45.md:229–245`). He reaches seven on boards and uses the public suite's push, lane, give, spark, and deep read (`manuscript/chapter-45.md:253–285`). Lira says she will tell nobody and permits only the costs, not the exchanges, in the Log (`manuscript/chapter-45.md:301–325`).

**Governing text.** The plan expressly orders “Night spar, Cael and Lira, closed floor (she asks for the rate he never shows)” (`BOOK_MAP.md:352`) and says the private floor is where the circle is honest with its bodies (`BOOK_MAP.md:425–426`). M7 repeats: “the closed floor, no witnesses; she decides to say nothing” (`packets/MOVEMENT-007.md:24`). The restriction is only that “The band's ceiling is never exceeded in front of the school” (`packets/MOVEMENT-007.md:36`); M8 still owns the Norhold rates and beyond “shown on the bluff for the first time” publicly (`BOOK_MAP.md:288–293`; `packets/MOVEMENT-008.md:25`).

**Ruling.** A closed hall with one trusted witness is not “in front of the school.” This is the planned private rehearsal of the lid, not a theft of M8's public reveal. The session is honest-floor canon: priced first, actual costs carried, no score, no Anchor deployment, no Shadow use.

### 8. Seln's five — RATIFY AS FORESHADOWING, NOT THE VOTE

**Page.** Seln lays out five beans for two watches and says only, “I was speaking of arithmetic” when Lira notices the fifth (`manuscript/chapter-42.md:143–155`). Cael later writes, “Not deciding, every day, on purpose, by all five of us, with a date on it” (`manuscript/chapter-47.md:25`) and immediately reads the five as Seln's arithmetic, “not … a better word for it yet” (`manuscript/chapter-47.md:27`). At the movement's close, the narration is explicit: “None of them had said they were leaving. He did not think any of them had decided it” (`manuscript/chapter-47.md:247`).

**Governing text.** M7 says, “The decision may be felt; it is not made” (`packets/MOVEMENT-007.md:33`) and assigns the night-road exercise to Brom's counting (`packets/MOVEMENT-007.md:25`). M8 owns the actual council choice: “five hands, Seln's last” (`BOOK_MAP.md:266–272,285–287`; `packets/MOVEMENT-008.md:24`). M9 then requires five to cross the Line while Ephram stays (`packets/MOVEMENT-009.md:31`).

**Ruling.** The five is inside the plan. It makes the eventual company legible without taking the vote: Seln teaches the arithmetic of the five bodies presently on the exercise; Cael names their shared discipline of postponement; nobody declares departure. The hand-vote and Seln's last hand remain wholly M8's.

### 9. New minor canon — RATIFY ALL FOUR GROUPS

#### Iron Skin in use

**Page.** “It always began at the wrists”; the surface stops creasing and takes a dull grey under lamplight (`manuscript/chapter-45.md:37–39`). Under the bar, the load travels through forearms, shoulders, back, hips, heels, and floor (`manuscript/chapter-45.md:43–51`).

**Governing text.** Book 2 says Iron Skin practitioners “raise a hardness of the surface of the body at need” (`../book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-18.md:21–25`), shows a blow's weight going through Brom “into his heels” (`../book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-18.md:181–185`), and maps hardening over forearm, foot, chest, and shoulders rather than the whole body (`../book-02-iron-circuit/manuscript/chapter-20.md:193–201`). The §9 “Iron Skin watcher” restriction is an untouched person/thread, not a ban on describing Brom's Path (`BOOK_MAP.md:640`).

**Ruling.** The visible change is a compatible close description of already-established surface hardening and load routing. It does not identify or touch the reserved watcher.

#### The sigil frame “under load”

**Page.** Brom's schedule requires “a reading in the sigil frame, under load … in the presence of the three assessors” (`manuscript/chapter-45.md:11`), with the assessors to set the load at the title figure and a little over (`manuscript/chapter-45.md:19`). The practice frame is carefully distinguished: “A sigil frame … read what the practitioner's Path did under the weight. This one read nothing. It only pressed” (`manuscript/chapter-45.md:35`).

**Governing text.** Book 4 establishes the district frame and sigil (`../book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-60.md:69–77`) and a Path-specific advancement protocol of sequence, load figures, then frame (`../book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-60.md:77,115–137`). The closed M1 coordinator ruling fixes a standard advancement panel as three assessors plus a sigil frame (`STATE_LEDGER.md:152`).

**Ruling.** “Under load” is a permissible Iron Skin-specific extension of the already-canon advancement apparatus, not a replacement for Lira's Wind protocol. The page distinguishes the crude training press from the reading instrument and creates no conflict with Books 1–5.

#### Farm, orchard, gardener

**Page.** The south-slope farm, walled kitchen gardens, two orchards, stream, water meadows, track, and cottage are laid out at `manuscript/chapter-42.md:65–67`; the unnamed gardener has kept the house's trees for thirty years (`manuscript/chapter-42.md:101–105`).

**Governing text.** Halcenvane already has a kitchen garden in Book 3 (`../book-03-no-path-given/manuscript/chapter-17.md:9`; see also `chapter-15.md:231`). M7 is assigned a new open road-craft exercise and the author owns its exercises and weather (`packets/MOVEMENT-007.md:25,42–44`); the plan requires Seln's road-craft but fixes no contrary bluff geography (`BOOK_MAP.md:353,379–380`). The role remains unnamed as required by the packet.

**Ruling.** This expands an existing campus edge without displacing any mapped structure. The orchard and gardener are safe minor canon.

#### Withrow's four “release” letters

**Page.** Of thirty-four letters, four advise Withrow “to let you go” (`manuscript/chapter-44.md:225–229`), using “*Release*” and “*Come to an accommodation*” (`manuscript/chapter-44.md:233`); she answers all thirty-four herself (`manuscript/chapter-44.md:241–251`).

**Governing text.** M7 expressly requires “Withrow's stack of letters from other chancellors; she answers them herself” (`packets/MOVEMENT-007.md:29`), and the supporting-cast plan makes her independent choice to answer part of her arc (`BOOK_MAP.md:408`). The packet keeps the other chancellors unnamed (`packets/MOVEMENT-007.md:7`), and §9 imposes no further release limit on these institutional-pressure letters.

**Ruling.** Four advising release is within the author-owned content of the mandated stack. It sharpens the clean-door pressure without making Withrow release anyone, naming another chancellor, or changing the proceeding.

## Exact line fixes

**None.** No `old:` / `new:` manuscript block is proposed, so there is no old string requiring a uniqueness count. All nine audited items stand on the current text.
