editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-13.md-147-He came to the wool exchange at the first bell on the fortieth day, an hour before anybody else, because the rotation manual said the junior chair would be in its place an hour before the sitting, and he had been in his place an hour before every sitting he had ever been posted to. He laid out the junior chair's things on the records table in the manual's order. First the minute-book, opened to a clean leaf. The receipt book for exhibits. The door log's copy, which the officer at the side door would send in with the porter at the half-bell, to be checked against the list. Two pens. Sand. A clean blotter. He squared each one to the table's edge.
