editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-12.md-6-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-12.md:7:The manual lay between them where Gault had left it, with the spill from the stove still marking its page. Cael opened it there and turned it round so that the print faced her. It was a page about thirty in from the front, in the part that set out the panel's duties. Most of it was the dry business of slates and seating and what a judge should do if a fighter fell into the trestle. But near the foot of the page there was a rule in its own short paragraph, set a little apart, as the oldest rules in a book are often set, and under it, in a smaller type, the office's annotation.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-12.md-8-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-12.md-9-He put his finger under the rule and read it aloud.
