editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-12.md-132-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-12.md-133-He could spend it now. If he did, he would have the touch in this exchange and very likely the next, and the bout would be over in four. A touch to one, then two to one: a quick clean win against a respected Iron Rank Nine of the guild, on the old man's own floor, before his own people. Cael could see what a panel would write for that. Execution high, because it would be clean. Control high, because the siege would turn in a single beat. Effect very high, because it would have taken a master's floor away from him in front of his apprentices. He could feel the figure it would make, and it was not a figure the band he had chosen was built to hold. It was the kind of figure the gallery would carry home and talk about, and the talk would reach the next meet before the wagons did, as the ganger's *undersold* had reached this one.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-12.md-134-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-12.md-135-Or he could hold it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-12.md-136-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-12.md:137:He could let the third exchange run out with nothing in it, and spend the light in the fourth, and take the second touch in the fifth. Five exchanges, hard-won, on a shrinking floor, against a local Rank Nine everybody respected, would rate as a strong Iron performance a sensible distance under par. That was the band. That was the figure a careful boy with good feet would earn.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-12.md-138-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-12.md-139-The cost of holding it was another whole glass on a floor that was getting smaller.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-12.md-140-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-12.md-141-He held it. He went on refusing, on worse ground than before, with the racks at his shoulder and the ropes closing, and he paid for every refusal on the contested boards in a way he had not paid in the first two exchanges. The yard-master's steps came closer together. Twice Cael had to turn hard off his left foot to keep out of the old man's reach, and the second time something in the back of his left calf drew tight, a long thin pull from the knee to the heel, like a cord being wound. He felt it and noted it and kept moving. He did not burst. He did not touch. He gave the panel a third exchange in which a boy kept himself alive on two-thirds of a floor and did nothing else.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-12.md-142-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-12.md-143-The glass ran out. "Even."
