editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-12.md:171:The fifth exchange was the yard-master finding out whether it had been luck.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-12.md-172-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-12.md-173-He had rebuilt his stance in the interval. He stood quartered now, his left shoulder turned toward the east, so that the high bright bar would fall beside his face and not into his eyes, and he came forward on a new line that kept the light on his left hand. It was a good adjustment. It was a fast one, too, for a man who had stood one way on that floor since before Cael was born.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-12.md-174-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-12.md-175-But a man cannot rebuild a lifetime's stance in the space of a glass, and Cael could see where the old stance was still in him. Every third step, the yard-master's feet forgot what his shoulders had decided, and squared up to the floor as they had always squared, and for that step the light was in his eyes again.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-12.md-176-
