editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-12.md:189:"The light from it." The yard-master shook his head. "Twenty years. I've had apprentices fall over in that light, and I've told them to stop being soft." He let go of Cael's hand. "You took a fifth exchange you didn't need."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-12.md-190-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-12.md-191-Cael did not answer.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-12.md-192-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-12.md-193-"Don't fret. I'll not tell anybody. It's your bout." The old man looked at him with a kind of grudging respect, as he might look at a beam that had held more than he'd thought it could. "You fight like an auditor. You go through a man's books until you find the one line he never checked, and then you sit there and wait until it's due."
