editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-43.md-71-He would read the figure. He would set his own two lines beside it, and he would find, as one always found, that the figure made the lines look a little different. A little smaller, or a little larger, or a little less certain. He had read figures for forty years. He had never once read one without letting it lean on whatever he had seen before it.
