editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-39.md-154-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-39.md-155-In the fourth he solved her, and Cael watched him do it and could name every step, which was very little comfort.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-39.md-156-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-39.md-157-The lattice-breaker had spent three exchanges learning the economics of a fighter with no frame, and in the fourth he stopped paying any of her prices. He did not take her open doors. He did not chase the places she walked past. He simply set his pushes so that every contact she made had to come through one of them first. Each raw ignition reached him thinned by the push it had passed through, and cost her palm every bit as much as before. She was paying the whole price for half the heat. Her invention was spent, and she had no frame left to fall back into. He took three points in a row, steadily, without any hurry at all.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-39.md-158-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-39.md:159:Nought to three, closed early. Five to nine. She could not have drawn level with the fifth, and it was over in four.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-39.md-160-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-39.md-161-She gave him her wrist to shake, as she had given it to Ivenne. He bowed to her a little, which nobody from Auremont did, and said something that made her nod.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-39.md-162-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-39.md-163-She came up the steps to the rail with her hands held out in front of her, and Gault had the water ready. The figures came up later: the lattice-breaker twenty-seven, Karis twenty-three.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-39.md-164-
--
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-35.md-254-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-35.md-255-The third came at the end of a long, close, fierce run along the north rope, with Lira a single stride behind her all the way and gaining, a hand's breadth and then another, so that for a moment the whole hall thought she would get there. She was almost there. The flag went up as her hand closed on the air where Zerin's shoulder had just been.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-35.md-256-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-35.md-257-"One to three," called the clerk, and then: "Four to nine on the bout."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-35.md-258-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-35.md:259:Nobody needed the sum explained. Lira could not have drawn level with every point of a fifth exchange, so there would be no fifth exchange. It was over in four.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-35.md-260-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-35.md-261-Zerin did not press.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-35.md-262-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-35.md-263-She had not pressed past what the bout required all morning, and she did not do it now. She stopped where she was when the flag went up, a yard from the rope, and let her hands drop, and turned to face the girl who had just spent everything she owned trying to catch her.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-35.md-264-
--
