editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-02.md:3:His registry name was on the fifth line, and it took him a moment to know it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-02.md-4-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-02.md-5-*Caelen Hesk-ward, 41-7843-V, enrolled practitioner; see demonstration provision, enrollment basis, filed.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-02.md-6-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-02.md-7-The entry instrument lay open on the records-hall counter with its four companions stacked beside it, and Bracken had turned it round so that Cael could read it right way up, which he did not need. It was the fourth day of term, a Fifth-day, in the hour after the morning's session let out, and the stone hall was empty except for the three of them and the clerk at the far desk, who was copying something with his tongue between his teeth. Bracken had sent a boy to the residence with the first bell, carrying a half-sheet that said *Records hall, the hour after session. Bring nothing.* Nobody else had been asked. Withrow had declined to come down, and had said so to Bracken in Cael's hearing in the covered walk the afternoon before, in five words: "It's a filing. File it."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-02.md-8-
--
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-02.md-62-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-02.md-63-"Be the regional registrar," she said.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-02.md-64-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-02.md-65-"What?"
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-02.md-66-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-02.md:67:"You're him. You've just broken the seal. You've read down to the fifth line and you don't like it, and you've got fourteen days and a clerk and a copy of the charter." She closed the notebook on her finger. "Find me the step. Find me the one place in this morning where somebody did something you can object to. Go on. You're better at hunting gaps than anybody I know."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-02.md-68-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-02.md-69-He thought about it. It was a fair request, and she would not thank him for being kind.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-02.md-70-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-02.md-71-"The enrollment," he said. "I object to the enrollment. The boy should never have been let in."
