editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-54.md:131:He ran it eight times. The first three came free, and he did not hide it. On the fourth he let the work show, as Brom had told him to, and came off the brass with his chest heaving and stood there breathing through his open mouth while all four chairs and three faculty watched. Best of the morning was the fifth. His legs told him so a count ahead of the clerk. The drop was cleaner and the held breath trimmed by a sliver that his lungs never noticed and the pendulum did, because a term of four hours a week on this oak had taught his leading leg to accept a landing instead of quarrelling with it.
