editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-54.md-209-It was not a spark, and it was not a letting-go either, though that was what it felt like at the very end; those were both the outside of it. Underneath both lay a choice of an instant. There is an instant when the unburnt turns into the burning, and what he had taken from her let him put that instant wherever he wished at the surface of his own skin. It was a decision point. Everything that came after it was the copper's business, or the water's, or the wood's, and only the moment was his. That was why it would not hold, and why it cost him the same whatever size he asked for, and why each one had to be made new: you could not hold a decision, you could only make another one.
