editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-29.md-218-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-29.md-219-He read them in order and in full.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-29.md-220-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-29.md-221-He had read a great many files in forty years, and most of them told him more about the office that had made them than about the thing they described. These were no different. Their courtesies told him which officers liked one another and which did not. Their dates told him that somebody had been in a hurry in the cold term, and then had not. He noted that, and did not make it carry more than a date can carry.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-29.md-222-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-29.md:223:There were the liaison papers between the observation office and the tournament's adjudication office, courteous and dull. There was the rotation of the observation officers through the three weeks, with their seats and their hours, and the names on it were names he knew. He found Ilsev in the first seat, as he had expected, and Havel in the second. There were the instructions that had gone out under each paper, and the acknowledgments that had come back. He read them all and found nothing in them that a careful office would not have written.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-29.md-224-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-29.md-225-Then he came to the seating of the Compact's row in the Concourse gallery.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-29.md-226-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-29.md-227-It was a plain document, a plan of twelve seats drawn in a clerk's neat hand, with a name-plate entered against each. The six observation seats came first, then the district officers, then a liaison seat, then two left for officials of the host city. He read along them in order. One, two, three. Eleven.
