editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-38.md:23:Along the row to her left, Havel was writing something small in the margin of his attendance sheet. Beyond him the other seats were emptying for the midday interval. At the end of the row the twelfth chair stood empty behind the grey rail-cloth, as it had stood every day of the sitting, with its pale square and its two empty screw holes. Ilsev did not look at it. She had a referral of her own still pending somewhere above her desk, the second she had ever filed on a single matter, and it had taught her a great deal about the difference between what a record said and what a record was for.
