editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-14.md-271-Then the river was behind them, and the road ran north and west, and for ten days it did not stop climbing.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-14.md-272-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-14.md:273:The first three days were farm country, rolling and wet, with sheep in the hollows and woodsmoke lying flat along the valleys in the mornings. On the fourth day the hedges gave out and the walls began, grey drystone walls that ran up the hillsides and over the tops, ruler-straight. On the fifth the farms got smaller and further apart and the inns got colder, and on the sixth morning there was ice in the horse trough at the waystation for the first time.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-14.md-274-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-14.md-275-Brom broke it with his fist and declared it a fair fight.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-14.md-276-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-14.md-277-Cael rode the tailboard, as he always did, and spent the long climbing hours on the line he had added under the costing. He tried the escape from Brom's hold in his head on every floor he had ever fought on: the wool town's pine, the mill hall's hard boards, the Ardenmere circuit's packed earth, the wash-house stone at Greyvane where he had once fallen badly and been laughed at for a week. On every floor that gave, the step worked. On every floor that did not, he could not make it work, however he turned it, and on the eighth morning he stopped turning it and wrote in the Log only *the step needs the floor's help; slate won't give it; find another answer before the bout or bring the feet back sooner.* It was not a solution, and he knew it was not.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-14.md-278-
