editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-50.md-35-On the long table lay the one book that was not a founding volume. It was the directive, all forty-one pages of it, the copy Bracken's clerk had made and a librarian had sealed, and down its fore-edge stood a row of small green paper tabs.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-50.md-36-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-50.md-37-Karis opened it without sitting down.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-50.md-38-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-50.md:39:"You know the green ones," she said. "Bracken put them in. One for each place in the whole directive where it takes an old word out of the law and puts a new word in." She ran her finger down the four of them, just above the paper. "And this one," she turned to a page with a white slip pinned to it and a thread of red through the slip, "is the transitional article. Havel read it out this morning. It's what won."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-50.md-40-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-50.md-41-She laid one forefinger on the last green tab and the other on the white slip, and held them there, as people hold two fingers on a map to measure a road.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-50.md-42-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-50.md-43-"Count the articles between," she said.
