editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-03.md-226-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-03.md-227-He had won. He knew that. Wray had called it, and Quenna had written it, and two hundred people had seen it, and nobody could take it off the record now. He also knew how close it had been. He had won one of the eight exchanges on a call that could have gone the other way. He had won another on a burst he had promised himself he would not spend, which had landed half an inch short of where he put it. And he had lost a third to a man who had learned his best read in one exchange and handed it back to him empty.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-03.md-228-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-03.md-229-There were two ways to write it down. He could feel both of them sitting in the pen.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-03.md-230-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-03.md:231:The first way was easy and true in every word. *Edran, exhibition, week twenty. Won four to three in the eighth exchange. Found the recovery in the off arm in the third; corrected in the fifth; pivot on time.* Every word of that was so, and it would read beautifully a year from now, and it would be a lie by omission. A lie by omission was still a lie, and he had built his whole life since Denvash on not telling any.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-03.md-232-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-03.md-233-He dipped the pen and wrote the other one.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-03.md-234-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-03.md-235-*Edran. Week twenty. Full sanction, Wray presiding, Quenna second seat. Eight exchanges.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-03.md-236-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-03.md-237-*Margin: one exchange. Possibly none. The sixth was a palm against an unburst shell arriving together. Wray called it for me. I can't say she was wrong. I can't say she was right either. If it goes the other way I lose.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-03.md-238-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-03.md-239-*What he built: he gave up a hand of reach to carry the off arm forward and close, so that his recovery is already in place when the lead bursts. Every read I had arrived correct and half a beat late. A correct read that comes too late is a wrong read that costs more.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-03.md-240-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-03.md:241:*Correction (found in the third, broken in the fourth, mended in the fifth): don't read the shell, read the off arm's shift. Then wait to see whether the arm follows, because he'll feed me the shift empty. That costs the time I was trying to save. It's still the only read that worked.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-03.md-242-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-03.md-243-*Wind: four bursts in eight exchanges. One over my ceiling. I spent the fourth knowing what it would cost, because nothing else was going to get there. It landed half an inch short and the landing beat ran long, longer than I've ever felt it. Hip: the line, crest to inner knee, tonight already, on boards. Expect three days.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-03.md-244-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-03.md-245-*Without the fourth there's no eighth exchange, so I'd pay it again. That isn't the same as saying it was cheap. I went over my own ceiling to win a bout I'd promised myself I'd win inside it. Against somebody better, one burst over wouldn't have been enough, and I'd have been two over, or three, and still lost.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-03.md-246-
--
