editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-11.md-77-She built slowly, as she had been told, and she counted under her breath while she did it, a low murmur like somebody reciting a lesson. "One, and set." Her right hand closed in the air a little above the height of her shoulder, as if she had taken hold of a thread hanging there. "Two, and set." Her left hand closed lower and farther out, almost level with Cael's forearm. "Draw." Between the two hands, for an instant, as she moved her head, he saw it: a line in the air, finer than a hair, catching one of the slanting bars of window light the way the strand of a web catches light in a hedge in the early morning, and then gone again when the light moved off it.
