editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-45.md-143-"Lira," said Rooke.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-45.md-144-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-45.md-145-He said nothing else. Lira nodded without looking at the sheet. There was nothing on it that she did not already know by heart, and she had already lost to the woman whose name was at the top of it, honestly, in front of eight thousand people, and she was going to spend everything she had learned.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-45.md-146-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-45.md:147:Rooke picked up the fifth sheet.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-45.md-148-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-45.md-149-He did not read it. He turned it over and laid it down again, blank side up, and rested his palm on it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-45.md-150-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-45.md-151-"I've no plan for her," he said.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-45.md-152-
--
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-45.md-170-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-45.md-171-"The Halcenvane squad, Chief Adjudicator," said the clerk. He was a tall careful man with ink on his second finger, and he held the slip as if it might go off. "Filed at dusk with the chancellor's signature. Five names and the rule's number. Nothing else."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-45.md-172-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-45.md-173-Umber put down his cup and took the slip.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-45.md-174-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-45.md:175:He read the names, and knew all five. He had watched two of them on the main floor from behind this very screen, and he had read the figures of the other three every evening for two weeks, and the fifth name had been on his desk in one form or another since the fourth morning of the sitting, when the trial rules went up on the board and he had read the rule's single sentence and known exactly what somebody would do with it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-45.md-176-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-45.md-177-The trial office's two senior clerks had come up the stair behind the first, and his own deputy behind them, and they stood in a row at the end of the long table and waited. Somebody had put the rules on the table already, open at the page.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-45.md-178-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-45.md-179-Umber read it aloud, once.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-45.md-180-
--
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-25.md-1-# Chapter 25 — Six and Nothing
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-25.md-2-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-25.md-3-The tenth session fell four days after the ninth, and Lira climbed the ladder again.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-25.md-4-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-25.md:5:It was not a bout anybody would remember. The name four places above her belonged to a Stone fifth-year who had stood near the top of the Copper sheet for two seasons by being very hard to move. He was not hard to move for long. Lira took him in three exchanges and three bursts, each touch arriving from a side his weight had just left, and the yard, which a fortnight ago had gone silent at eleven seconds, clapped politely and went back to its bread and its arguments. That was a measurement too, and Cael wrote it down: a thing that had been impossible on the second session had become, by the tenth, merely expected.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-25.md-6-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-25.md-7-He did not count the man at the edge of the lamp. He did not look for him in the brass. It was the fourth day without, and every hour of the four had cost something, a small steady tug at the corner of his eye like a loose thread he kept wanting to pull. He let it be.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-25.md-8-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-25.md-9-That evening Brom fed him at the post.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-25.md-10-
--
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-25.md-60-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-25.md-61-"It's the standard I'd hold anybody else's read to." He closed the Log. "And the next time I need it, I don't think it'll be at a post."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-25.md-62-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-25.md-63----
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-25.md-64-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-25.md:65:Lira went down at the fifth bell because her hip woke her at the fourth.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-25.md-66-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-25.md-67-It had been doing that since the Mire boy. Not pain, exactly: a heat, a line drawn from the crest of the bone down the inside of the thigh, faint and bright, as if somebody had laid a warm wire under the skin and forgotten to take it out. It woke her before the bell and would not let her lie on that side again. So she got up and dressed in the dark, quietly, because the residence walls were thin and Cael, two doors along, slept like a man waiting to be called. Then she went down through the sleeping residence and out into the grey.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-25.md-68-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-25.md:69:The first morning, three weeks ago, she had only meant to walk the hip loose along the covered walk. She had found the standings clerk already at the board under the north stair with his chalk and his ladder, chalking yesterday's results in a silence so complete it seemed to be part of his work. Nobody else was there, and nobody else, it turned out, ever was. Forty people would stand in front of that board by the seventh bell. At the fifth there was the clerk and the chalk and the cold, and now there was Lira.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-25.md-70-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-25.md-71-She had told nobody. It was not a secret; it was simply hers.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-25.md-72-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-25.md-73-The clerk was finishing the Copper column when she came round the corner of the stair. He nodded to her, as he nodded every morning now, the nod of one early riser to another, and went on chalking. She took her usual place, an arm and a half back from the board, and let her hands hang.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-25.md-74-
--
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-25.md-84-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-25.md-85-*Copper.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-25.md-86-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-25.md-87-She watched him chalk it, as she always did.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-25.md-88-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-25.md:89:There had been a hall at Fenmark with a long table and a woman behind it who had spent about a minute and a half on her, and at the end of the minute and a half had written a word and a number: Copper, Rank One. That was three years ago. Since then Lira had fought on a foundry floor and on the Ironyard's planks and in a formal yard at Greyvane, and in front of eight hundred people here, and she had beaten fighters whose paper said Iron. She had a flag on her own file, put there by somebody who had once looked at her properly, and the flag had gone into a queue, and the queue was where it still was. And every morning at the fifth bell the clerk chalked the same word beside her name as he had chalked the morning she arrived.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-25.md-90-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-25.md-91-The morning after the final, the same hand would chalk the same six letters. No bout on any floor could reach it. Only an advancement evaluation could, and nobody had ever sent for one. It was not a thing bouts were for. She had known that on the road down to Ostrand, and she had come anyway, and climbed anyway, and here she was, at the top of a column that could not move the one word she wanted moved.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-25.md-92-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-25.md-93-She noticed, after a while, that she was standing with her weight square on both feet.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-25.md-94-
--
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-25.md-98-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-25.md-99-She did not turn round. She did not need to. The third step from the top of that stair had a board that complained under any weight, and every person on the bluff trod on it and made it complain, except one. He stepped over it. He had stepped over it since his first week, without thinking, the way he stepped over everything he had once noticed, and so she had heard the second step and the fourth step and no third. Then nothing at all.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-25.md-100-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-25.md-101-He was standing at the top of the north stair, looking at her looking at the board.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-25.md-102-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-25.md:103:She kept her eyes on the chalk and her breath where it was, and she could feel him reading her. She could feel him knowing, from forty feet, everything she had not said, everything she did not want said at the fifth bell in a cold covered walk with a stranger's chalk-dust in the air. If he came down the stair now she would have to turn round and have a face. She did not have one ready, and she did not want to build one.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-25.md-104-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-25.md-105-The stair was quiet for a long time. Then, very softly, she heard him go back down it. The fourth step, the second, nothing on the third, and then the flags of the lower walk, going away round the long side of the lecture quadrangle, where nobody went at that hour because it was the long way to anywhere.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-25.md-106-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-25.md-107-Lira let her breath go.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-25.md-108-
--
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-25.md-122-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-25.md-123-Brom looked from one of them to the other across the table, and opened his mouth, and then, with the air of a man stepping carefully over a third stair, shut it again and passed Cael the bread.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-25.md-124-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-25.md-125-The challenge was posted on the residence board by noon. Cael read it with his coat still on.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-25.md-126-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-25.md:127:A name four places down had sent its challenge up to her for the eleventh session, and the name was one he knew well, because he had been writing it down for most of the season. A Shield Path fifth-year, Copper Rank Six on his card. Bracken's files gave him forty-one wins across five seasons on the Copper sheet. Nobody on the bluff could remember him losing a bout that mattered, and nobody could remember him winning one beautifully either.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-25.md-128-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-25.md-129-Cael had seen him fight six times. He went back to his room and took out the page and read it again, slowly, the way he would have read it the night before a bout of his own.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-25.md-130-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-25.md-131-*He does not attack. He edits. His Path declaration is the pane. He sets it a hand's breadth off the forearm, wider than his shoulders, and plants his feet, and then he waits, and the floor gets smaller every second he waits. He doesn't win bouts. He stands there while you lose them. Forty-one wins and I can't find one where he struck first.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-25.md-132-
--
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-25.md-136-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-25.md-137-He read it twice. It was a good page, with six bouts' worth of evidence under it and he could not find a hole in it anywhere.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-25.md-138-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-25.md-139-All the same, he went down to the practice rings the next afternoon to look at the man once more, because a page was a promise and he liked to see a promise kept before he handed it to anybody.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-25.md-140-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-25.md:141:The Shield fifth-year was on the second ring with a Current second-year who had asked him for a bout, which happened, Cael gathered, rather often. The older students sent their juniors to him to learn what it is to strike something that does not move. He fought the second-year just as he fought the ladder. At the instructor's word he took a long breath, and planted his feet one after the other, and the pane came up glassy and wide off his left forearm, and then he waited. The Current girl waited too, for a while, politely. Then she tried to ride round the pane's edge on her own current, and he turned it a hand's breadth, no more, and she went round it into nothing and sat down on the boards.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-25.md-142-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-25.md-143-He helped her up and said something to her, quietly, and pointed at her feet. She nodded and went back to her chalk and tried again, and sat down again, and laughed.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-25.md-144-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-25.md-145-Cael wrote the bout in the margin of the page. *Same as the six. Breath, plant, pane, wait. She waited too.* He underlined *wait*, because it was the heart of the man, and closed the binder. He had been looking for a hole in the pane. He did not think to look anywhere else, and he would think about that for some time afterward.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-25.md-146-
--
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-25.md-154-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-25.md-155-From Lira, that was not agreement. He knew it was not, and he did not press, because it was her bout.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-25.md-156-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-25.md-157----
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-25.md-158-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-25.md:159:The eleventh session drew a good crowd for a Copper card. The yard had decided Lira was worth climbing the tiers for, and it had also decided, in the refectory queue and on the lecture stair, that this was the afternoon she would finally be made to wait. Everybody knew the Shield fifth-year. Everybody had lost money, or a bet of honour, on somebody being certain they would get through his pane.
