editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-44.md-16-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-44.md-17-The eve went into the Log by the low lamp.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-44.md-18-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-44.md:19:*Three days of a whole hill not doing anything. It's harder than doing something. I know, because not doing anything is what I've done on my own account for two years, and I've never once seen anybody else try it. Withrow's bet is that a visit can only see what's been changed for it. So she's changed nothing, and made five hundred frightened people change nothing with her. I didn't expect the first person I ever watched play my own game to be a chancellor, and play it better.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-44.md-20-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-44.md-21-*Bumps: one, in hall three, the one Brom caught. Otherwise none for three days, and I've paid for every one. I know now what free feels like. Free is the thing to be frightened of.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-44.md-22-
--
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-44.md-24-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-44.md-25----
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-44.md-26-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-44.md:27:Havel rode up the bluff road in the second carriage, facing backward, with the counsel's knees an inch from his own and the counsel talking.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-44.md-28-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-44.md:29:She had talked since Ostrand bridge and, if he was honest, most of the way from the coast, four days of it. Her voice was low and pleasant and never rose and never stopped, and it went on about the inns, and the state of the road, and a case she had argued nine years before in which a schedule had turned out to have two different pages numbered eleven. She did not need answers. She needed somebody to aim at, and Havel, facing backward with his case on his knees, was the nearest thing in the carriage with ears. He had learned by the second day to nod at the end of her sentences and think about something else in the middle of them.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-44.md-30-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-44.md:31:Beside the counsel, Assessor Ilsev sat with her own case on her own knees and her eyes shut, though she was not asleep. Havel had sat beside her for four days now, and through the whole of the Greyvane hearing before that, and he had never once seen her asleep. She shut her eyes in carriages the way other people shut a book they had finished with, so that nobody would ask her what she thought of it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-44.md-32-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-44.md:33:The carriage leaned into the first bend of the bluff road, and Havel looked out of the window at the river falling away below.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-44.md-34-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-44.md-35-He did it properly. He had been trained to arrive at a place properly, and he did it now out of a habit so old it no longer felt like a decision. First the road behind them: the bridge with its toll board, the town, the ferry landing on the near bank with its lamp post and two men in road-grey coats standing under it. He noted the two men, his own service's coats, briefly and without staring, and looked away again, because a records officer who stares at a rotation from a carriage window is a records officer who will be asked by somebody, later, why. Then the road ahead: the bends, the wall, the gate. Then the slope of the hill itself, the roofs above the wall, the long range of grey stone along the top of the bluff with its tiers of seating, open to the sky, standing up against the morning like the side of a ship.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-44.md-36-
--
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-44.md-38-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-44.md-39-He knew who would be up there.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-44.md-40-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-44.md:41:He had known it since the coast. He had sat at the end of the delegation's table at Greyvane through every sitting, with his recorder's case open and his brass glass turning, and written down every word the boy said. That had included all of the schedule the boy had read aloud in a level voice that stopped at the end of every entry for as long as a pen needed. Before that there had been a cold bright morning in the Unranked District at Ardenmere, a pump step, a form with four questions on it, and a fifth question that had not been on any form. And a pear, held out in a twist of paper, that he had wanted and had not taken. He still did not know why he had not taken it. He had thought about it more often since than a man ought to think about a pear.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-44.md-42-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-44.md-43-A boy like that, on a morning like this, would want to see the delegation come in. He would want to see it from somewhere legitimate, where nobody could say he had no business to be. He would want a clean line down the last of the road and across the courtyard. He would want to be high, and empty, and early.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-44.md-44-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-44.md-45-The upper tier of the Crown yard, then. The east end of it, most likely, where the morning sun would be behind him.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-44.md-46-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-44.md:47:Havel looked at the plan of it in his head. Then he decided not to look up there.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-44.md-48-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-44.md:49:It was a small decision, and he made it the way he made most of his decisions now, quickly and without enjoying it. A man who looks straight at the place where he knows someone is standing has told that person something: that he knew, and that he thought about it on the road, and that the knowing mattered to him. Havel did not want to tell the boy any of that. He was not sure it was true. He was not sure it was his to tell. And there was a correct way to arrive at an inspection, which was to look at the building, and the door, and the officer waiting at the door, and the ground. He would do it the correct way.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-44.md-50-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-44.md-51-The carriage came up the last bend, and leveled, and went in under the gate.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-44.md-52-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-44.md:53:He had risen twice since the pump step, and he thought about that sometimes in the evenings. The first rise had come not long after Ardenmere. He had been taken off the district rota and given a recorder's case and a seat at the end of hearing tables, where the work was to write down exactly what was said, and nothing else, and to be thought careful. He had been thought careful. The second had come after Greyvane, and it was the one that had put him in this carriage. Records officer to a delegation was a grade that sat at the main table and not the end of it. It was a grade that handled the delegation's papers before the senior seat saw them.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-44.md-54-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-44.md-55-Neither rise had come with an explanation, because promotions in the service did not. A sheet arrived with a new grade on it and a date, and you moved your desk.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-44.md-56-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-44.md:57:Some things on the boy's file stood above his clearance, and he did not think about those in carriages. He had a notebook with a cardboard cover in his inside pocket, and in it, among dates that meant nothing, there were three short entries in pencil that he had written over two years and did not read in front of anyone. He did not take it out now, since there was nothing to add to it this morning. There might never be. A careful man kept a notebook like that for the reason he kept a spare button sewn inside his coat: not because he expected to need it, but because when you needed it, there would be no time to sew.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-44.md-58-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-44.md-59----
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-44.md-60-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-44.md-61-The carriage stopped.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-44.md-62-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-44.md:63:The counsel was still talking as she gathered her gloves. Havel opened the door for her and got down first and held it, and she came down the step after him, talking. On the cobbles of the outer courtyard she stopped, and looked up at once at the roofline of the long building in front of her, the one with the stone hood over its door.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-44.md-64-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-44.md-65-"That gutter," she said to the clerk who had come across from the first carriage, "has been mended three times, and badly, and the third mend is newer than the second. Write it down."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-44.md-66-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-44.md:67:The clerk wrote it down, and Havel did not look at the gutter.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-44.md-68-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-44.md:69:He looked at the courtyard. He took it in the way he had been taught, the corners first and the centre last, because the corners are where people stand who do not want to be in the centre. The north-west corner, with a water butt and a boy sitting on an upturned crate with a bucket at his feet. The north-east, where the covered walk came out under an arch, and a knot of perhaps twenty students were standing about with the elaborate unconcern of people who had found a reason to be crossing a courtyard at this hour. The south corners, empty, swept, with the morning sun lying across the cobbles. Then the door of the long building, under its stone hood, and the man waiting at it: a man in a dark coat with ink on one cuff and a pen behind his ear, standing very straight, with a younger clerk at his elbow. The registrar, Havel thought; he had read the name in the papers. Then the ground, the cobbles between the carriages and the door, which were clean and dry and had not been swept that morning, because the dust along their edges was a day old.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-44.md-70-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-44.md-71-He did not look at the long grey wall of the Crown yard rising above the courtyard's east side. He did not look at the tiers along the top of it, or at the east end of the upper tier where the sun was. He knew where it was. He kept it at the very edge of the side of his eye, where a thing is not looked at but is still known, and he kept his face toward the door.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-44.md-72-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-44.md:73:It was harder than it sounded. Every instinct a records officer owns runs the other way, toward looking, toward the high place and the far corner and the window with a shape behind it. He had learned at a desk by a window, with a manual open in front of him, that a correct officer and a careful one are not always the same man. A correct officer looks at everything he can see. A careful one decides which of the things he can see he is going to be seen seeing. For most of his life Havel had been correct, and been praised for it. For some time now he had been trying, with no instructor and no manual, to be careful as well, and he was still not sure that he was any good at it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-44.md-74-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-44.md:75:His right hand ached in the web between the thumb and the first finger, as it always did at the end of a long road, from years of pens. He flexed it once against the handle of the case and kept walking.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-44.md-76-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-44.md:77:Behind him the second carriage creaked as Ilsev got down. He heard her feet on the step and then nothing, because she had stopped on the cobbles and was standing still, as she always did when she arrived anywhere, while everything round her moved. She did not need help with her case. Nobody offered any. In four days Havel had never once seen anybody offer, and he suspected that the last person to try had been very young.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-44.md-78-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-44.md-79-Then the third carriage.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-44.md-80-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-44.md:81:Havel did not turn round for it, because he did not need to. He heard the clerks from the first carriage go across the cobbles toward it at a quick walk. He saw, without looking straight at him, the ranking man of the escort move four paces to his left, unbidden. He heard the counsel, beside him, stop talking in the middle of a sentence about the gutter, for the first time since the bridge.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-44.md-82-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-44.md:83:He had ridden behind that carriage for four days and eaten in the same inns, at a different table. He had stood in the same yards while the horses were changed. And he could not, if anyone had asked him on oath, have said one thing about the man inside it beyond his rank and his name, because the man had given him nothing to say. That was not a complaint. Havel had simply noticed it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-44.md-84-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-44.md-85-He walked toward the registrar's door with his case held close against his side, at an ordinary pace, looking at the door.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-44.md-86-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-44.md:87:The registrar met him on the step. He was not what Havel had pictured from his signature on the enrollment record: he had a pen behind his ear and ink along the edge of one cuff, and he did not bow.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-44.md-88-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-44.md-89-"Records officer," he said. "You'll want the enrollment basis first, I expect."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-44.md-90-
--
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-44.md-92-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-44.md-93-"Of course." The registrar inclined his head. "Whenever she asks, it's on a shelf in my outer office. Four boxes, with an index on top. I'll have a clerk carry them to your table."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-44.md-94-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-44.md:95:In a good many years of arriving at institutions he had met a great many registrars, and nearly all of them had told him first, at the door, how difficult the records were to find. He had never before been told at the door where a file was and how many boxes it filled, by a man who seemed to think that was the ordinary thing to say. It was the manner, he thought, of somebody who had built a thing for strangers and was quite content to let strangers have it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-44.md-96-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-44.md:97:"Thank you," said Havel, and meant it, and wrote nothing down.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-44.md-98-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-44.md-99-Inside, under the stone hood, the hall was cool and dim and smelled of old paper and lamp oil, and his footsteps went ahead of him along the flags. He let himself think, once, about the high tier above the courtyard behind him, and the east end of it with the sun on it, and who was standing there. He did not turn round. There would be days on this bluff for looking. This was not one of them.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-44.md-100-
--
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-44.md-124-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-44.md-125-Then the man who had held the carriage door for her.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-44.md-126-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-44.md:127:Cael knew him before he had properly looked. It was the way the man held his case: flat against his left side, close, with his hand spread across the lid, holding it shut. A young assessor had held a case just like that, crossing a square at Ardenmere toward a pump step, on a bright cold morning that felt a great deal longer ago than it was. The young assessor had stopped being young, and his coat fitted him better than it had. There was a newer mark at his collar than the one he had worn at Greyvane.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-44.md-128-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-44.md-129-He walked across the courtyard toward the registrar's door, and he did a thing that Cael noticed only because he was looking for something else.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-44.md-130-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-44.md-131-He did not raise his eyes.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-44.md-132-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-44.md:133:Not at the clock over the arch. Not at the roofs. Not at the long grey wall of the Crown yard or any of the tiers along its top. He looked at the corners of the courtyard, one and then the next, the water butt, the arch, the swept empty south side, and then at the door and at Bracken in it, and then at the cobbles, and he kept his chin level the whole way across. Nobody does that in a strange place. Everybody arriving anywhere new looks up at least once, at the roofline, at the height of the walls, at the sky. Havel walked the whole width of the outer courtyard like a man walking under a ladder he has made up his mind not to notice.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-44.md-134-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-44.md-135-"That's the pear one," said Lira. She had not been looking at the courtyard; she had been looking at Cael's face, and she must have seen the man arrive there.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-44.md-136-
--
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-44.md-162-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-44.md-163-And the count went wrong.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-44.md-164-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-44.md:165:It did not come back empty. He knew empty; empty was a number too. It came back arguing with itself. In two years of counting rooms, his method had given him too little and too much and once, at Ardenmere, nonsense. It had never before given him three answers at once that would not agree.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-44.md-166-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-44.md-167-*One.* The courtyard leaned. The two clerks from the first carriage were crossing toward the third before its door was fully open. The ranking rider of the escort moved four paces to his left without anyone telling him to. The counsel, at the records hall's door, stopped in the middle of a word about the gutter and stayed stopped. The whole courtyard leaned toward the third carriage. Cael had seen people do that round Withrow every day of the season, and he could have drawn it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-44.md-168-
--
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-44.md-180-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-44.md-181-Cael's hands were flat on the cold stone of the rail.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-44.md-182-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-44.md:183:He read the man anyway, with everything he had. An empty answer only counts if the question was really asked. He went through every layer of the gaze, the way he had with each watcher in turn since the very first. There had been Coss on a road and Coss in a hall, a young assessor at a pump step, the open coats on the Greyvane road who had let themselves be counted, a copying clerk at a gallery rail whose eyes went everywhere except to Cael.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-44.md-184-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-44.md-185-Rhythm. The step down from the carriage and the steps across the cobbles came at the same pace. Not tired, then. Not hurried. Nothing more.
