--
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-44.md-194-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-44.md-195-It was not carelessness. Carelessness has a slackness to it, and there was nothing slack anywhere in that man. It was a reader running his finger along a page at one even speed from the first line to the last, so that nobody watching the finger could ever tell which line he cared about.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-44.md-196-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-44.md:197:He had been doing a rough cousin of it himself for two years, sampling crowds he could not count. But he sampled to save effort. This man did it so as to give nothing away.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-44.md-198-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-44.md-199-The last clerk went in under the stone hood, and the door shut. The riders led the horses off toward the stables, and a boy ran out for the baggage. In about the time it takes to tie a boot, the outer courtyard was only a courtyard again, with the sun lying across its swept south side.
