editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-46.md-7-Rooke passed him at the arch, going the other way, with a sheaf of cohort sheets under his arm and the rain dark on his shoulders. "Enrollee," he said, as he had said it every day for a month, and not a syllable more. Cael said "Instructor," and they went on, and it was nothing at all. Cael found he was grateful for it all the same, as a man is grateful in a strange house for one chair that stays where it was.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-46.md-8-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-46.md:9:Behind Rooke came two first-years, walking very close together and talking very low. They were discussing whether the Archmarshal could see through walls. One of them had heard it from a cousin. The other was not convinced, but was walking a good deal closer to the inside wall than the rain required, as if she were giving the matter the benefit of the doubt. Cael let them pass and did not smile, because smiling in the walk this week would be noticed by somebody, and a noticed smile was a story by supper.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-46.md-10-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-46.md:11:He saw Havel at about thirty feet.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-46.md-12-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-46.md-13-The records officer was in the westbound stream, coming toward him along the wall, with a flat document case held close under his left arm and the clerk who carried the lists half a pace behind him. He walked at the pace of a man going to a sitting he was not late for, with his head level and his eyes on the door at the far end of the walk.
