editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-46.md-32-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-46.md:33:Havel had seen the boy at thirty feet and had known him at twenty, by the walk.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-46.md-34-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-46.md:35:He would have known that walk anywhere. He had watched it come across a square at Ardenmere toward a pump step, on a cold bright morning two years ago, when he had worn a worse coat and been, he sometimes felt, a good deal younger than two years should account for. It had changed since, grown longer in the stride and lighter on the heel, and it had stopped hurrying. But it still took the middle of whatever space it had been given and no more than the middle, as if somebody had once explained to the boy how much room he was allowed and he had believed them.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-46.md-36-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-46.md:37:The delegation's card was in Havel's inside pocket, beside the notebook. It was a small stiff card, printed, and every officer on the manifest had been handed one at the coast. Officers of the delegation did not converse with any party under review outside a sitting. They did not acknowledge any such party beyond ordinary courtesy. They did not carry messages, take papers, or accept anything offered.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-46.md-38-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-46.md:39:That last line had made him put the card down on the inn table, the first time, and look at the wall for a while. It had not been written for him. It had been written years before he was born, for every officer of every delegation, by somebody who had never heard of a pump step. He knew that perfectly well. He had read it as if it were about a pear all the same.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-46.md-40-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-46.md-41-He read the card again on the second night, the way he read every instruction, once for what it said and once for what it left out. What it left out was the thing he had thought about, in the end, for most of a stage between two inns.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-46.md-42-
--
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-46.md-44-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-46.md-45-So there was an ordinary courtesy. The card granted it by forbidding everything past it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-46.md-46-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-46.md:47:Fifteen feet. The boy's eyes were on the walk ahead, not on him, as they should have been. Havel kept his own on the door, because he did not want to see the face. If he saw it, he would read it, and if he read it, the boy might read him reading, and then there would be a conversation in that walk with not a word in it. The card forbade conversation.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-46.md-48-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-46.md-49-Nine feet.
