editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-46.md-53-They passed. He did not feel the boy's sleeve. That was the point of the inch.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-46.md-54-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-46.md:55:He straightened. Behind him the clerk with the lists said something about the rain, and Havel answered something about the rain, and the door at the end of the walk came up and opened and took them in.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-46.md-56-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-46.md-57-Not until he had sat down and opened the case did he let himself think about it, and then only for a little while, the way he thought about most things now, sideways, so as not to be seen thinking.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-46.md-58-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-46.md:59:There had been a pear, once. He had not taken it, and he still could not say why not. He thought now that he might have been afraid it would cost the boy something. A boy seen giving a pear to a district assessor would be a boy somebody asked questions about afterward. So he had refused a kindness to save the boy the price of having offered it, and had walked away with the boy's face still in front of him, puzzled and not offended, and had carried the puzzle for two years.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-46.md-60-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-46.md-61-The inch did not make up for the pear. Nothing would make up for the pear. But it was within his instructions, and it was the whole of what was within his instructions, and it had cost the boy nothing at all, because nobody in that walk could have seen it but the boy.
