editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-46.md-68-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-46.md-69-It took him three tries to write it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-46.md-70-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-46.md:71:The first time he wrote *avoidance*, and struck it out, because the man had not been avoiding anything. The second time he wrote *manners*, and struck that out too, because manners were what you did with your hat and your voice, and Havel had done nothing with either. The third time he gave up on finding a word for it and simply put down what had happened, in order, as Karis would have wanted.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-46.md-72-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-46.md:73:*Covered walk, rain, both streams jammed against the dry wall. Havel westbound with a case and the list clerk. Read clean: an officer going to a sitting, nothing aimed my way. Then, nine feet out, his near side drew in. Shoulder down and forward, a little more than an inch. He held it all the way past. He made his own stream narrower so that mine would be wider.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-46.md-74-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-46.md-75-*He wasn't getting out of my way. I was nowhere near his way. He was making room.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-46.md-76-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-46.md:77:He turned back to Havel's own page and read it through again, top to foot. The pump step. Four questions on a form and a fifth that was not on any form. The pear in its twist of paper. The recorder's case at Greyvane, and the brass glass turning at the end of the table. *He keeps going up a grade, and he keeps turning up wherever I am.* And at the very bottom, in Lira's square capitals, because she had taken the pen out of his hand one evening in the common room and written it herself:
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-46.md-78-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-46.md-79-*Careful men go up. Doesn't tell you what he's careful FOR.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-46.md-80-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-46.md-81-He sat with that for a while.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-46.md-82-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-46.md:83:He still did not know who kept moving the man up, or why every move seemed to set him down in some room Cael was standing in. There were things in Havel's work he was not allowed to say. Cael had always been able to see that much, because the man wore it in his face like a collar a size too small. The walk had told him nothing about any of it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-46.md-84-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-46.md:85:What the walk had told him was smaller, and he had known it for two years and never quite let himself keep it. Whatever the man had been ordered, he had always done every scrap of kindness the orders left room for, and then stopped at the line. At the pump step it had been a fifth question asked in an ordinary voice. Today it had been an inch.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-46.md-86-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-46.md:87:Cael had gone back and forth for two years over whether that deserved thanks. A part of him had always said that thanking somebody for so little was a kind of foolishness. It said that a boy who was grateful for an inch was a boy who had got used to being given nothing.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-46.md-88-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-04-copper-crown/manuscript/chapter-46.md-89-He looked at the struck-out words, and at Lira's capitals, and found that the argument was over.
