editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-25.md-34-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-25.md:35:He had been a monitoring officer for most of his working life, and the first thing a monitoring officer learns is to watch for the moment when a pattern changes. A man who has crossed a certain bridge every morning for ten years and one morning takes the ferry has not done anything unlawful; he has only done something new, and the new thing is worth writing down in a ruled book. Havel had written down a great many such things about a great many men.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-25.md-36-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-25.md:37:He did not write this one down. There was no fifth entry. The rule of the notebook was that it held what had happened and nothing of what he made of it. He had broken that rule once, on the bluff, and did not mean to make a habit of it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-25.md-38-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-25.md-39-But he noticed, standing there, that he had changed his pattern. He noticed it as precisely as he would have noticed it in anybody else, and then he went to bed without unpacking the kit to put it right.
