editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-25.md-249-Cael's own line on the board meant the Shield reserve, every second day, on the sprung oak, for the whole of the block.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-25.md-250-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-25.md:251:The Shield reserve was a third-year from a farming family in the river country, broad and fair and slow to speak, with a habit of apologizing to the floor when he slipped on it. He had been told in the first week of the year that he would travel with the squad and fight for nobody, and he had taken it without complaint. Now he had been told that he would spend ten weeks letting the provision boy take his coverage apart. He took that without complaint too, and asked only whether he might keep a note of what the provision boy did, so that he could learn something from it himself.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-25.md-252-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-25.md-253-"Keep it," said Rooke. "Show it to me."
