editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-58.md-34-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-58.md-35-She opened the bottom drawer of the desk, where she kept her own things, and took out a sheet of paper that was not the office's. It was a sheet she had bought for herself at a stationer's on the coopers' row on her second day in the city, for letters home, and never used. She laid it on top of everything else, over the office's paper, and she wrote on it. She did not write in her office hand. She wrote in the hand she had used as a girl at her mother's table, before anybody had taught her to make her letters stand up straight.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-58.md-36-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-58.md:37:What she wrote, nobody ever read, because it was not for any file and not addressed to anybody, and it was the first thing she had written in all her years of service that was not meant to be read by somebody else. She wrote it, and read it through once, and folded it twice, and put it inside her coat, against her chest, where she had never in her life carried anything that belonged to the Compact.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-58.md-38-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-58.md-39-Then she finished the routing minute on the front sheet, correctly, under the right article, and sent the file on up the line as the charter required.
