editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-58.md-98-
editions/monroe-1.3/book-05-the-silver-standard/manuscript/chapter-58.md:99:He noticed that before he had written a word, and it was strange enough that he put the pen down and sat for a moment with his hands on the blotter. Every report he had sent north in a year had been built: true in every line, complete in every box the form asked for, and arranged with great care so that nothing in it led anywhere. He had built them as other men build walls, a stone at a time, checking each. This one did not need building. There was nothing to keep out of it. Eight thousand people had watched the thing. Three scribes had written it down at a speed no clerk could match. The west tower had put it in eleven words and hung them in a frame. Whatever he wrote, the world already held more of it than his form had room for.
