editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-42.md:103:Seln had been to see him that morning. He was a wide slow old man with earth in the creases of his hands, who had kept the house's trees for thirty years. He had been told that four young people from the hill would come walking up to his door, one at a time, and that he was to stand at his window and look at each of them exactly as he would look at a stranger coming down his track at dusk. He had asked whether he was allowed to say what he thought, and Seln had said that was the point.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-42.md:151:He looked at the five beans for a moment.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-47.md:25:*That's a decision too. I'm entering it as one. Not deciding, every day, on purpose, by all five of us, with a date on it.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-47.md:27:He had looked at *five* for a while after he wrote it, and then he had let it stand. Seln had counted five beans on a gardener's table and called it arithmetic, and Cael did not think there was a better word for it yet.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-47.md:133:Cael had not seen it out of that pocket since the day it came. It was square, of a heavy cream paper, a little rubbed at the corners now from eighteen weeks of being carried against a man's ribs. The seal on its flap was a dark red wax with a device pressed into it that he had never been close enough to read. The seal had not been broken; there was not so much as a crack across it.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-47.md:139:"Four weeks," he said. "To the day. I worked it out on the box, coming down, last Fifth-day, after the hood said it." He did not look round. "I thought I'd like to stop carrying it."
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-47.md:243:None of them had said they were leaving. He did not think any of them had decided it, in so many words, even alone. It was only that there were now three boxes on a shelf, and two under a bed, and a carter's book in a coat, and a new strap on a satchel, and five beans in a gardener's jar.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-47.md:253:*On Brom's table: one envelope, sealed, square to the edge. Four weeks now, less a few days.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-47.md:267:*Next: Ostrand. Two sittings and a ruling.*
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-47.md:269:He closed the book and put it in the satchel, in the pocket that had been cut for it from memory, and did up the buckle on its new strap. Then he sat on at the open window with the satchel on his knees, and watched the two small lanterns at the foot of the road until the bell went and two more came down to relieve them.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-44.md:189:He took out the six sheets.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-44.md:199:He had thought, this morning a week ago, with the dry pen resting under a fourteenth line, that there might be a seventh. He could admit that here. What he had hoped, for the space of perhaps a breath, was a simple thing: that a mark written out at last in a schedule, under seal, before a seat, would have to say whose it was. Every designation he had ever receipted stood against the office that made it, and in all these years he had wanted nothing from that mark but the name of an office.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-44.md:201:The schedule did not give one. It gave the title, in a clerk's fair copy, and no office after it, on the old leaf or the new. The petition had only set down that the mark was there. He had known that since the pump step, and had written it on the first sheet, and a thing written once did not want writing again.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-44.md:203:Everything else about the line, the registers answered perfectly. Second schedule, fourteenth line, received on the fortieth day, re-tendered on the hundred and seventeenth, both leaves alike. There was nothing there to write. The six sheets held what the registers could not answer. This, they could.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-44.md:209:He thought instead, for a while, of the words on his rotation order. *The seat's Fifth-days, until relieved.* He had read them in the first week as he read any order, for what they required of him. He read them differently tonight, in his head, with the river going by. A man could be relieved because the work was done. He could also ask.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-41.md:13:It took them until the light was going, a slow line down the switchbacks with the trunks swaying and the wheels loud on the turns, and at the foot of the road the coats stepped back against the chandler's wall to let them by. On the river the ferry made its crossing, and came back empty, and crossed again full. Four hundred and thirty enrollees lived on the bluff, and by dusk forty-one of them were left.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-41.md:15:Four of the forty-one had signed their slips together, at the wing's counter, that morning before they went down.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-41.md:19:It was the plainest reason of the four. Since the year's eighth day the registry had had a petition before its seat in the town below, asking that he be stood in a frame and read again. If the frame could not read him, the code had a chapter for that: his enrollment struck out, and after it, a letter at a time, everything else with his name on it. And a respondent who left the hill while the thing was pending would be entered as fled, with a note on three friends' files to say whose friends they were.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-41.md:53:Six people now knew what was in the case on Karis's shelf, and the sixth was an officer of the Compact who had read it through that afternoon and carried nothing away but the knowing. What it was, Cael did not write. It was not a thing that went into a book.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-41.md:65:The refectory had one table lit, for the cooks had put all forty-one of the house's remaining enrollees at the long table nearest the kitchen door, so that they would only have to carry the porridge one way, and the rest of the hall stood empty under its high windows with the benches turned up on the boards. The forty-one sat close together at their one table, as people do in a big room, and talked in low voices.
editions/monroe-1.3/book-06-the-compacts-hand/manuscript/chapter-41.md:93:"The hall keeps no recess," said Bracken, before he could ask. "Enrollees go home. Paper does not." He turned the daybook round on the counter so that Cael could read the morning's entry, which was a thing he did only when he was pleased with it. *Recess, the first day. In residence: forty-one enrolled, by slip. Post: the small bag. Nothing sealed.* "Nothing sealed," he repeated, and turned it back. "I have written that at the foot of the day for a week running, and I find I look forward to it. Go and be idle somewhere. It is what a recess is for, and I am told it does not come naturally to everybody."

