+## (4) Reader clarity
+
+**Pass, subject to line fix 1.** The chapter 41 additions give a fluent thirteen-year-old three usable anchors without explaining the secret: why Cael stays, what flight would record, and what “Six now” counts. The procedural distinctions in chapters 44 and 46 remain the movement's highest memory burden, but speakers, documents, and consequences are attributable on one pass. Havel's schedule logic is clear after one local consolidation: he hoped the sealed designation would name its office; it names only the title; the absent office is old information, so there is no seventh sheet.
+
+Dialogue attribution remains reliable in the multi-person reading-room, orchard, hearing, and night-floor scenes. Pronouns and physical referents survive the cadence joins: Seln is the reader of Hesk's address in chapter 41; Bracken is at the coach door in chapter 44; Vastin's clerk carries the tray in chapter 46; the final “two more” in chapter 47 unambiguously means relief watchers.
+
+The Reader Standard passes: no obscenity, sexual content, graphic violence, or cruelty-as-pleasure; institutional and physical stakes are concrete; moral choices cost the characters something; action remains trackable. The ending now favors objects over another abstract explanation and is stronger for both the younger and adult genre lenses.
